# The Case Against Travel

![rw-book-cover](https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6495d60f69af7f864913e415/16:9/w_1280,c_limit/Callard_final.jpg)

## Metadata
- Author: [[Agnes Callard]]
- Full Title: The Case Against Travel
- Category: #articles
- Summary:: The article argues against the idea that travel is an inherently valuable experience. The author claims that travel often turns people into tourists who go from one place to another seeking to experience something new, but who end up consuming pre-packaged sights and experiences that have been designed to appeal to their tastes. Traveling in this way can make people feel like they are in touch with humanity when, in fact, it alienates them from it. The author suggests that people should focus on understanding the places they visit on a deeper level instead of just ticking off a list of tourist attractions.
